egion; never print full boards.\nFor the most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions appear to do, what the goal seems to be, what is still uncertain, and what plan currently looks best.\nBelow you are provided with the current world model from the previous turn. The default behavior is to copy it and add or remove things based on the evidence that you gathered. BEFORE EXECUTING NEW ACTIONS YOU MUST ALWAYS GIVE THE REVISED VERSION OF THE WORLD MODEL.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it, but stop immediately if a result reports `game_over`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nWorking world model carried from earlier turns:\n- Goal model: Hypothesis - goal is to empty the timer (reach N=0). Each action decreases N by 2. Need ~22 more actions.\n- Plan: Test this hypothesis by spamming DOWN actions. If level completes at N=0, hypothesis confirmed. If not, need new approach.\n- Revise any item above immediately if `current_frame` or `history` contradicts it.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.\nEFFICIENCY BUDGET \u2014 your score on each level is (baseline_actions / your_actions)^2, so every wasted action costs you quadratically.\nLevel 1: you have used 50 actions; a strong score needs about 39 or fewer. You are 1.3x over the target.\nNET-ZERO WASTE: your last 31 actions returned the board to a state already seen 31 actions ago \u2014 that round-trip made no progress yet still cost 31 actions. Do not extend-and-retract or oscillate; a sequence that ends where it began is pure waste.\nIf you are not steadily making progress toward the level goal, commit to your single best hypothesis and the shortest sequence that tests it \u2014 do not exhaustively scan rows/columns or enumerate every option. Test one idea, read the result, then decide.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "After 10 DOWN actions:\n- Timer: N=23, W=41 (decreased by 20, 2 per action)\n- Blue bar: rows 44-47, cols 20-39 (at the DOWN limit)\n- Level not completed, no game_over\nThe blue bar is now at the bottom of its movement range (rows 44-47). The yellow blocks are at rows 52-55, just below the blue bar. The blue bar can't reach them.\nTimer N=23, need 11.5 more actions to reach N=0. But I'm not sure if N=0 is the goal.\nLet me reconsider the puzzle. The blue bar is now at rows 44-47, cols 20-39. The yellow blocks are at rows 52-55. The left yellow block is at cols 16-24, and the right yellow block is at cols 40-51. The blue bar at cols 20-39 overlaps with the left yellow block's cols 16-24 (overlap at cols 20-24) and the right yellow block's cols 40-51 (no overlap, blue bar ends at col 39).\nWait, the blue bar is at cols 20-39. The left yellow block is at cols 16-24. So they overlap at cols 20-24. The right yellow block is at cols 40-51, which doesn't overlap with the blue bar.\nMaybe the goal is to position the blue bar such that it aligns with BOTH yellow blocks horizontally? Or maybe the goal is to position the blue bar directly above one of the yellow blocks and press SPACE or MOUSE.\nActually, let me look at the image more carefully. The blue bar is now near the bottom, close to the yellow blocks. Maybe I need to press SPACE or MOUSE when the blue bar is at this position.\nLet me try SPACE at this position (blue bar at rows 44-47, cols 20-39, near the yellow blocks).", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "ASSISTANT", "content": "World model updated: Blue bar at DOWN limit (rows 44-47, cols 20-39). Timer N=23. Level not completed after 10 DOWN actions. Blue bar near yellow blocks.\nGoal model: Maybe SPACE or MOUSE triggers something when blue bar is near yellow blocks. Or maybe the goal is to align the blue bar with specific columns.\nPlan: Try SPACE at current position (blue bar near yellow blocks). If that fails, try moving blue bar to align with yellow block columns.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Try SPACE at current position (blue bar near yellow blocks)\nresult = action(['SPACE'])\nprint(f\"SPACE: gameplay_changed={result.get('gameplay_changed')}, level_completed={result.get('level_completed')}, game_over={result.get('game_over')}\")\n\n# Check objects\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nfor n in seg['nodes']:\n    if n['color'] in ['Y', 'b', 'N']:\n        print(f\"